INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-024508be-1872-47c1-9f04-e60f9cac6256', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792469895759, "durationMs": 231495}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #150 round 4 is clean on `9fe5dbfd`. The merger can now trust the size lines in the PR body. The head did not move during the review. I checked it at the start, before posting, and at the end.\n\n- **Round 3''s minor is fixed.** The \"File caps\" bullet now says `asks.rs` 255 and `ask_gate.rs` 128. `git show 9fe5dbfd:<path> | wc -l` gives the same numbers. The \"grows by 14\" for `mono_messages.rs` matches its +47/-33 numstat.\n- **The other counts in the body match the head.** I measured each one this session:\n  - Both line counts from `scripts/gate/integrity.sh`: 401 for this PR against `f808e4af`, and 212 for part 2 against part 1. Part 2''s branch is still at `a747d808`.\n  - The diff sizes: +829/-48 for the PR, +203/-98 for the round 1 fix, and +22 for the round 2 fix.\n  - The 63 non-test lines in `ask_gate.rs`.\n  - The finding counts the body gives for rounds 1 and 2.\n  - The schema ranges and the budget defaults and bounds.\n  - The mutation-table tallies.\n- **Not re-run.** The brief allowed no heavy runs, so I did not re-run the builder''s old test totals or the session counts of the database copies. Those are past results, and none of them contradicts the head.\n- **Two nits, so the verdict stays clean.**\n  - The body says the `mono_messages` table has \"40\" CHECKs. On the head it has 52: 36 table-level, 15 column CHECKs, and one in `PIN_TURN_ID`.\n  - \"Review rounds: 2\" is behind, because this head has now had rounds 3 and 4. It could become 4 when the PR merges.\n\n  Both nits a"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-14675861-3ce7-4d2a-a256-966589a0543f', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792440167312, "durationMs": 2044169}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Round 1 on #139 needs changes: 0 blockers, 1 major, 4 minors, on head `119c58d17eadd81d9ae46384bd82250be2929bd5`. The head did not move during the review. The review is in `change-r/reviews/r4-round1.md` and is posted as the summary comment, along with 7 line comments. `review/agent` is set to failure on that SHA, and the nits went to https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/issues/140. No model spend.\n\nThe Rust work is right. No call, live entry or app action carries a copy of a session''s lead any more, and a call that still sends one is refused when it arrives. A session''s level is read only from its saved row. The new rank comparison gives the same answer as the old rule on every case the gate can reach (a table in the review shows each one). CI is 14 of 14 green, and the PR merges cleanly onto today''s `tyler/main` (`b608de65`).\n\n**The major (M1).** A session can have app access and still have no saved row, so real callers get `unknown_caller`. This is the rule-of-premise problem the brief asked about.\n- **Refused forever.** The app never saves a session whose folder is `~`. That is the first tab when there is no project. A typed `/operator` still gives that session app access.\n- **Before and after.** I ran the same check on old and new code. An unsaved `/operator` caller in `~` calling `sessions.list` got `Allowed` on `tyler/main` and gets `unknown_caller` on this head.\n- **Web side.** A throwaway vitest shows the app''s own code skips saving that session and still "}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-1875ed49-c3f9-4673-9613-68319940ed2a', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792435188258, "durationMs": 2052611}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I reviewed PR #138 at `6e9e4c877733db598b2ba343658b6658e0df6bb1`. The verdict is changes-needed: 0 blockers, 0 majors, 1 minor and 4 nits. The flake fix itself is correct. The minor is that the spec does not prove the pause behaviour the PR says it covers. https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/138\n\n**Cause.** The claim holds. I recounted every probe job from the raw logs. Two video tabs failed 15 of 100 runs across H.264, VP9 and VP8. One video tab failed 0 of 60. The two groups differ only in the second tab. All three original CI failures timed out at 15000 ms with no value returned, which fits a hang, not a slow decode. One supporting point in the PR body is wrong (NIT 1). It says the hang depends on the two players starting 6 ms apart rather than 30 ms. In 73 of 85 passing two-video runs the players also started within 9 ms. The real difference is that a hung run stops logging halfway. So a product fix that only delays one video''s load would not help. The \"1 in 17 000\" odds mix codecs that fail at different rates. Counted per codec it is about 1 in 1,300 (NIT 2), and the conclusion still stands.\n\n**Does the test still prove its claims.** Decode, play and seek, yes. Pausing on a tab change or a hidden workspace, no, and it never did on tyler/main either. The clip is 3.0 s long. If the app never pauses it, the clip plays to the end inside the 5 s check, and a finished video reports paused. I removed the app''s pause code, and then the hidden-workspace wiring, i"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-2021673b-9cca-4b18-af29-cc89128e2bb6', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792438799273, "durationMs": 272941}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #138 is clean at `2cf5e48f7e5edfc79c05882f673932aee97e0e00`, with 0 blockers, 0 majors, 0 minors and one new nit in the PR body. The merge gate is open for this exact commit. A push after it needs a new review.\n\n**Minor 1 is fixed.** With the head''s `element.loop = true`, I re-ran both round 1 mutations locally on chromium and webkit. I ran them one at a time under `heavy-lock.sh` with `--workers=1`, on this spec only.\n- With the app''s pause code removed from `BinaryFileView.tsx` (lines 207 and 271), the spec fails at line 50 with `Expected: true, Received: false`.\n- With `visible &&` removed at `FilePane.tsx:219`, it fails the same way at line 68.\n- The unmodified spec passes on both engines.\n\nIn round 1, both mutations passed. The files were restored after each case.\n\n**The new 20/20 run is real.** Run 39309810707 tested commit `2b45b0bc`. Its only parent is `2cf5e48f`, and it adds only the scratch workflow file. My recount shows `5 passed` in each of the 4 webkit jobs, 0 failed, and no `15000ms` in the log. The scratch branch has been deleted. After the first test on each runner, tests took 2.6 to 2.7 s, against 1.6 to 2.3 s in round 0. I did not find why, but every test passed well inside the 5 s poll.\n\n**Nits 1 to 4.**\n- **Nit 1, timing claim:** fixed in the body. The fixture comment now says \"in one\" instead of \"starting together\".\n- **Nit 2, odds:** fixed in the body. My recount gives 1 in 1,274 and 1 in 9,910, which matches the body''s \"1 in 1 300\" and \"1 in 9 900\"."}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-27522563-01ba-4381-bada-ae3b30f5be8b', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792426888507, "durationMs": 1599427}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "All of round 1 on #127 is fixed in one push. The new head is `83b0ba76cadf77251f807de53c08dfe5b58ff419`. I applied no ruleset and changed no setting. It is not merged.\n\n**CI.** Every check passes except `gate/integrity`, which fails as expected. It printed `edits a gate file` for 6 files (`gate.yml`, `eslint.config.js`, `integrity.sh`, `integrity.test.sh`, `review-status.sh` and `ruleset.json`), then `integrity: 6 finding(s). Each needs a fix, or an override from Tyler for a gate PR.` `gate/lint` passes and prints `ok` for all 33 probes. tyler/main moved to `b809501f` (#126) during the round. The PR still merges cleanly, so I did not rebase.\n\n**B1: the verdict could land on an unreviewed head.** The new usage is `review-status.sh <pr> <reviewed-sha> <state> <summary>`. The script reads the PR head once, and posts only if the PR is open and its head equals the reviewed SHA. Otherwise it exits 2 and posts nothing. I ran the reviewer''s `status_probe.sh`, adapted to the new argument with 40-hex SHAs:\n\n| Case | Exit | Output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Reviewed `aaaa…`, head moved to `bbbb…` | 2 | `PR #127 head is bbbb…, not the reviewed aaaa…; review the new head and post again`, no POST |\n| Head unchanged | 0 | posted `success` on `aaaa…` |\n| PR closed | 2 | `PR #127 is CLOSED`, no POST |\n| SHA `aaaa` | 2 | `reviewed SHA must be 40 hex characters`, no POST |\n\nAt the old head `a8aa082f`, the first case exited 0 and posted on `bbbb`.\n\nI also ran it for real on this PR. Passing the old"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792429234314, "durationMs": 2869789}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "ADE-10 is fixed in PR #134, which is open and not merged. The head is `58bddd6c8ebc41a0f7271e6076459f34be5baed6`, on tyler/main `e5ac68fb`. CI passes 14/14, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity` included; `gate/integrity` reported `ok, 50 non-test lines changed`.\n\nWith this fix, a queued Mono message that starts after a notice gets its real start time, its \"Worked for\" duration, and the right place in ADE-5''s project ranking.\n\n**The fix.** `turnOpener` now returns the turn-opening message that started last, not the last one in the chat. It still skips steers, waiting queued messages and drafts. A message saved with no start time still counts at its place, which keeps an existing ADE-7 test passing. `runningTurnOf` had its own \"latest start\" loop; it now reads the turn id from `turnOpener`, and I deleted the loop. One pure function now decides the opener for all four readers: the live-agents panel, `stampTurnDuration`, ADE-5''s `runningTurnStart` and the composer bar.\n\n**Callers.** The LSP has no server for this repo, so I found them with grep: `toLiveAgent`, `stampTurnDuration` and `runningTurnStart` for `turnOpener`, and `SessionPane` for `runningTurnOf`. The PR body has the before/after data flow.\n\n**Before and after on the real turns.** I loaded the Fork Orchestrator chat from a `.backup` copy of the `com.monocode.second` DB through the real loaders:\n- **Rust side:** a throwaway Rust test called the real `mono_session_get` and `mono_session_page` code on the copy.\n- **TypeScript"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792432170411, "durationMs": 2983234}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "ADE-49 is fixed in PR #138, which is open and not merged. The head is `6e9e4c877733db598b2ba343658b6658e0df6bb1`. CI passes 14/14, including `browser`, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity`.\n\n**Cause.** It is not the codec. The test''s fixture opens two video tabs, `clip.mp4` and `other.mp4`. `FilePane` keeps every file tab mounted, so both `<video>` elements start loading at the same moment. In Playwright''s Linux WebKit (WPE with GStreamer), two video pipelines that start together sometimes hang the whole web process. The evidence:\n- **The failed traces.** In all three I read (#102, #109 and #81), the poll''s first `locator.evaluate` found the video and then never returned for 20 s, until the test ended. A slow decoder would have returned \"not ready\" and let the poll retry. Here the page itself stopped answering.\n- **The browser log of a failing run.** It ends with `WebProcess didn''t exit as expected after the UI process connection was closed`. It also shows the two media players being created 6 ms apart with no decoding after that. In a passing run they are 30 ms apart and decoding starts 43 ms later.\n- **Codec experiment.** A scratch workflow ran the spec on webkit, 5 times on each of 4 runners per variant:\n\n| Fixture | Second tab | Failed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| H.264 mp4 (today''s) | video | 2 of 40 |\n| VP9 WebM | video | 9 of 40 |\n| VP8 WebM | video | 4 of 20 |\n| VP9 WebM | image | 0 of 20 |\n| H.264 mp4 | image | 0 of 40 |\n\nEvery codec fails with two videos (15 of 100) and none"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792437269531, "durationMs": 1512311}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "The pause checks in #138 now actually test the pause, and the PR is ready for the next review. The new head is `2cf5e48f7e5edfc79c05882f673932aee97e0e00`. CI passes 14/14, including `browser`, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity`, and the PR merges cleanly. It is not merged.\n\n**Minor 1, fixed in 2cf5e48f.** The spec now sets `element.loop = true` when it starts playback, with a one-line comment saying why. I ran the reviewer''s two mutations locally under `heavy-lock.sh`, on chromium and webkit:\n\n| Case | chromium | webkit | Where it fails |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Unmodified spec | pass | pass | |\n| Pause code removed (`!visible` in both places in `BinaryFileView.tsx`) | fail | fail | tab-change check, spec line 50, `Expected: true, Received: false` |\n| Hidden-workspace wiring removed (`FilePane.tsx:219`) | fail | fail | hidden-workspace check, spec line 68 |\n\nBefore this round both mutations passed on both engines.\n\n**New 20/20 proof.** [Run 39309810707](https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/actions/runs/39309810707) ran the new head, plus only a scratch workflow file, on webkit: 4 runners × `5 passed`. I deleted the scratch branch and its worktree.\n\n**Nits.**\n- **Nit 1:** the Cause section no longer makes the 6 ms vs 30 ms timing claim. It now says a hung process stops after its first pair of GL sink warnings, while a passing one logs a second pair, and that the gap at start is 2 to 9 ms in both.\n- **Nit 2:** the odds now use each codec''s own failure rate. That gi"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792439166527, "durationMs": 5111908}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #141 is open with all 14 CI checks passing. I have not merged it.\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| PR | https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/141 |\n| Head SHA | `b368e39fc78f9f0a8935b0fce35ac1c788841679` |\n| Base | `tyler/main` at `b608de65` |\n| CI | 14 of 14 pass, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity` included |\n| Size | 36 non-test lines (`monoSessionCompletion.ts` +25 −8, `App.tsx` +3). Tests are +188 −5 across three files |\n| Review rounds | 0 |\n\n**Cause.** A steer from `sessions.send` attaches its watcher to the turn that the `sessions.start` request already watches (`src/app/model/appSessionSend.ts:191`). When that turn ends, `submitSession` gives the same outcome to both watchers (`src/app/App.tsx:8634-8635`, which calls `appSessionSend.ts:314`). Groups are keyed by Mono turn (`monoSessionCompletion.ts:182`), so the two requests sit in two groups, and each group sends the result on its own (lines 201-202, `release` at 252). The only duplicate check is by message id (line 353), and every group gets a new id (line 266), so the second copy gets through.\n\nThere was a second fault in the same report. The reply text stopped at the next user message (line 44), and the steer counts as one. So the first report said \"Now open the PR.\" instead of the turn''s final answer. That is the same text as real notice seq 1892.\n\n**Fix.** Each watcher now settles with the session end it reports, and App passes the turn''s outcome as that end. The first watcher to settle records "}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792445910769, "durationMs": 6462260}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I fixed round 1 of #141 in one push on top of current tyler/main. All 14 CI checks pass and I have not merged.\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| PR | https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/141 |\n| New head SHA | `f60c19e6d796fdc0f0a7c1beddc2fc966851aa4d` (rebased onto `d29fea87`) |\n| CI | 14 of 14 pass, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity` included |\n| Line comments | answered both: `discussion_r4319787944` (MAJOR) and `discussion_r4319788146` (MINOR) |\n| PR body | rewritten for the new design, with the new data flow, mutations, size and \"Review rounds: 1\" |\n\n**MAJOR fix.** The settle step no longer claims an end or removes anything from a group. Every request stays in its group with its result and its end. The pure `releasableResults` makes the one decision about what a group sends. It skips a ready result when its Mono already got a report of that end, and it sends only one report per end within a single release. `release` marks an end as reported only when it actually hands the report to `onReady`, and that can only happen after the request is accepted. A skipped request still leaves its group, so the group never waits for it.\n\n**MINOR fix.** `watch(origin, request, options)` now returns a watcher that takes `(outcome: ControlOutcome)` and nothing else, and builds the report itself. App passes a session lookup to the constructor. `monitorCompletion` in App.tsx is now just the `watch` call, and the old wrapper is deleted. The tests call `watch` the same way App do"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792453542316, "durationMs": 2005320}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I fixed round 2 of #141 in one push. All 14 CI checks pass and I have not merged.\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| PR | https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/141 |\n| New head SHA | `f7248a05559533549ac8b09d3eb0b12ca915e9a4` |\n| CI | 14 of 14 pass, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity` included. The PR is `MERGEABLE` |\n| Line comment | answered (`discussion_r4320122393`) |\n| PR body | \"Review rounds: 2\", plus a round 2 note, the new mutation rows, the corrected Tradeoffs line and the new size |\n\n**MINOR fix.** I added one test to `monoCompletionBatches.test.ts`, \"reports the steered turn''s final answer from the open session\":\n- The batches get a real lookup that returns session `s`.\n- `s` holds a start, the early reply \"Now open the PR.\", the `send-s` steer (`sentAt`, no `startedAt`), the final reply \"PR #103 is open.\", and then the next turn.\n- Only the `send-s` steer watches the turn. Its receipt id is not its session id. It goes through the real `createTurnWatchers` and `deliverAppSteer`.\n- The turn ends with outcome text \"control\". The test checks that the one report''s `result` is \"PR #103 is open.\".\n\n**Mutations** (four test files, 59 tests pass on the head):\n\n| Mutation | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| M-d: `session: undefined` | 1 fails: the new test |\n| M-e: `session: this.session(requestId)` | 1 fails: the new test |\n| 2. One report per end across all Monos (after the N1 change) | 1 fails |\n| 7. A delivered end is never recorded (after the N1 change) | 4 fail |\n|"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792473481531, "durationMs": 1087939}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "The design is done: one cause, a one-rule fix plan, and two frames on a new doop page. The fix only changes the footer''s time value, so a waiver request fits. The ticket''s example is also overstated, which matters for how much this is worth.\n\n**Cause.** The footer takes the end of the *last* run of the merged Mono message, not the run that *started* last.\n- `src/features/sessions/ui/AgentTranscript.tsx:1075-1078` sets `lastRun` to the last run by position from `monoTurnRuns` (`transcriptActivity.ts:437`). Those runs are grouped by position in `groupMonoChatTurns` (`transcriptActivity.ts:415`).\n- `AgentTranscript.tsx:1613-1619` sets `completedAt = lastRun.startedAt + lastRun.durationMs`.\n- In an inverted turn, a queued message sits above a notice that ran before it. So the footer shows the notice''s end.\n\n**Real example, with true end times.** The ticket''s \"ran to 08:33\" was only an upper bound. The real ends come from the Mono''s own omp transcript (the last assistant message with `stop`):\n\n| Turn | Started | Real end | Footer when the turn ends, today → fixed | Footer in the full chat today |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `m_4kkwsmecdx` | 08:03:45 | 08:03:57.9 | 8:03 AM → 8:03 AM | 8:03 AM |\n| `m_ghsv7ogxwg` | 20:26:05 | 20:26:34.2 | 8:26 PM → 8:26 PM | 8:27 PM, a later notice''s real end |\n| `m_nrw3tsvkuxzg` | 21:33:51 | 21:34:10.6 | **9:33 PM → 9:34 PM** | 9:34 PM, a later notice''s real end |\n\n- In seconds, the wrong end is 12 to 29 s early.\n- The clock shows minutes only"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792480863575, "durationMs": 1544625}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #156 is open and all 14 CI checks pass. I have not merged it.\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| PR | https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/156 \"ADE-59: Mono turn footer shows the turn''s real end\" |\n| Head SHA | `b35fa0f60a469a28fda724bc9622ce01f6e8d7b7` (branch `fix/ade-59-footer-end`, on tyler/main `a363d977`) |\n| CI | 14 of 14 pass, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity` included. GitHub shows the PR as `MERGEABLE` |\n| Size | 12 non-test lines (+6 −6) in `AgentTranscript.tsx`. Tests are +56 in one file. `integrity.sh` prints `ok, 12 non-test lines changed` |\n| PR body | Linear link at the top, \"Review rounds: 0\", before/after data flow, gate checklist |\n\n**Fix.** The position-based `lastRun` pick (`AgentTranscript.tsx:1075-1078`) is gone. For a message with more than one run, the footer now takes the opener from ADE-10''s `turnOpener(turn)` and adds that opener''s `durationMs`. `turnOpener` is the same pure rule that `stopStreaming` uses to choose which block gets the turn''s duration (`stampTurnDuration`, `apply.ts:751`). So the footer now reads the duration from the same block that received it, and there is no second \"latest run\" rule. Single-run messages and habit reports keep today''s path.\n\n**Test.** I added one scenario to `AgentTranscript.inlineWork.test.ts` with two checkpoints:\n- the normal shape, where the message runs first and the notice after it;\n- the real inverted shape of `m_nrw3tsvkuxzg`.\n\nIt fails on tyler/main with `expected ''9:33 PM'' to be ''9:34 "}]');
