INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-385a239e-340c-4431-9dd8-23d877083e1b', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792434716236, "durationMs": 658082}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #132 is clean at d6596fc1. All four round 1 minors are fixed, and each fix held up when I ran it. There are no blockers, majors or minors. I filed one new nit (n8) on #135. The review is in `change-r/reviews/r2-round2.md` and is also posted as a PR comment. `review/agent` is set to success on d6596fc1.\n\n- **m1 (the lease flake).** The run that failed 4 of 1200 on aac6d80e now passes 1200 of 1200 (8 processes, 150 runs each, 31 s). The retry cannot hide a real lease bug. A mutant where a killed app never releases its lease still fails, after the 2 s retry. A second launch still fails on its first try, so a lease that does not block a second process is caught. The app itself still takes the lease once.\n- **m2 (real-DB copies).** The clock scenario now passes on a desktop backup copy that already has a `mono_clock` row plus an `app_down` row and a `sleep` row. The output confirms it ran on that copy (199 sessions). Fresh backup copies of both real databases also pass.\n- **m3 (stall logged as sleep).** Your stall-as-sleep mutant now fails with `a stall is no sleep`, left 4, right 3.\n- **m4 (lease tied to the clock).** Code outside the module cannot drop the lease of a running clock. That attempt does not compile (`E0616`, private field). Both mutants that start the clock after letting go of the lock fail the scenario.\n- **The two surviving mutants.** I agree they are nits. Nothing reads `boot_wall_ms`, so its mutant changes no behaviour. Whether to read the column or drop it i"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-3a5f2c37-9c77-45f3-8ee0-53f33ef47519', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792480837809, "durationMs": 457394}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #117 at 6831fef2 is clean. The code has no blocker, major or minor finding, and `review/agent` on 6831fef2 now shows success. It still can''t merge yet. `tyler/main` moved to ed381066 at 18:22, while I was reviewing, when #150 (the ADE-23 ask ledger) merged. The branch is now BEHIND. The ruleset requires branches to be up to date, so the branch needs an update, and that new head needs its own review.\n\nFor the user, the only visible change is the switch text in the milestones settings popover. It now reads \"Off stops agents from changing milestones here.\" The source line hashes the same as the plain string, and `od -c` shows only plain ASCII. The old \"Off hides the project\" text is gone from the tree; `git grep` finds no match at 6831fef2. It stays only as history in the PR body and in `decisions.md`.\n\n**Range-diff.** It shows the three round 2 commits with byte-identical patches (`=`), so every round 2 proof still holds. The new commit 6831fef2 changes one file, `MilestoneSettings.tsx`, with one line added and three removed. Those are the new text and the deleted doc comment \"with the frame''s text\", which had become false. The brief expected changes in tests and fixtures too, but no test or fixture ever held the old text. I confirmed that with `git grep` at 70c43f1d.\n\n**Spec and screenshot.** I ran `milestones-settings.spec.ts` once in chromium and webkit under the heavy lock with `--workers=1`, and 22 passed. `change-m/reviews/M-10-review-off.png` was taken at 17:03 AEDT, "}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-3e217e0f-f3fe-4289-aee1-dbee872c4aa7', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792475360318, "durationMs": 1814982}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Still waiting on another session''s lock, so the stale doc comment can be fixed before the run starts."}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792477177362, "durationMs": 14}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792480711547, "durationMs": 42972}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "#117 still needs no work: the wording change and the rest of the brief were finished before the Ship 5 swap. After the relaunch, everything still checks out on head `6831fef2`. I did not merge it.\n\n- **Head SHA.** The local branch and the pushed branch are both `6831fef2`. It is already based on tyler/main `a363d977` (`git merge-base --is-ancestor` passes), so I did not rebase or push again. The worktree has no uncommitted tracked changes.\n- **CI.** All 14 checks pass on `6831fef2`, including gate/lint, gate/integrity, browser and check on macOS, Ubuntu and Windows. GitHub shows the PR as BLOCKED. The ruleset still needs a review/agent status and resolved threads for this exact head, and no reviewer has looked at it yet. https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/117\n- **Screenshot.** It is at `/Users/tylerstewart/MonoCode/gigas/monocode-general-2/projects/change-m/reviews/M-10-review-off.png`, taken from lane 3 in chromium. I opened it and the popover shows the new text. Its md5 `8aded87e…` matches the test output it was copied from.\n- **Grep.** A search for `Off hides the project` over `src`, `tests` and `src-tauri/src` finds nothing. `Off stops agents from changing milestones here.` appears once, at `src/features/milestones/ui/MilestoneSettings.tsx:99`.\n- **Tests.** Before the swap I ran every milestones Playwright spec on this head under heavy-lock, chromium and webkit, `--workers=1`: 130 passed, 10 skipped. The skipped ones are the perf spec, which only runs on"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-4a5254b4-9bc7-4209-ae48-3f42697ae6a5', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792455563544, "durationMs": 319314}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #141 is clean at head `f7248a05559533549ac8b09d3eb0b12ca915e9a4`, with 0 blockers, 0 majors, 0 minors and 0 nits. The review is in `change-linear/reviews/ade47-round3.md`. I posted it as a comment on https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/141, and `review/agent` is now success on that SHA. When a session''s turn ends, a Mono that watched it from two requests now gets one report, and that report carries the turn''s final answer. The orchestrator can merge once Tyler''s other gates allow.\n\n- **The new test catches both mutations.** \"reports the steered turn''s final answer from the open session\" sends a real session through `watch` for the `send-s` steer. It checks the report''s `result`. With `session: undefined`, and again with the lookup done by request id, that test is the only one that fails. Both failures show the diff I predicted, `\"control\"` where `\"PR #103 is open.\"` was expected (`mut-de-diff.out`).\n- **N1 changes no reports.** \"No set\" and \"empty set\" give the same answer. Nothing runs between the decision and `reported.add`, so creating the set later opens no reentrancy window. I re-ran round 2''s eight race orderings once, and each still gives one report per Mono. I added a ninth probe, which confirms a Mono gets its set only when a report is sent. Two N1 mutations each fail 4 committed tests: the set never stored, and the stored set never read. So the stored set is still what stops a second report when two groups release separately.\n- **N2 is fixed.** T"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-4f6b36a5-b1df-4767-8aec-85214746761f', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792431709851, "durationMs": 1234118}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #132 needs changes at the pinned head `aac6d80e11fdd28a976b71afc807a021998dfa8e`. I found 0 blockers, 0 majors and 4 minors. The clock rules, the units for all three platforms and the lease all hold up. The one unexplained failure is now explained, but it is not fixed. https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/132\n\n**The failure.** I reproduced it. It failed 1 time in 400 runs and 4 times in 1200 runs when 8 test processes ran at once. The clock scenario alone passed 1200 of 1200. Every failure was `another process holds the lease` at the first `down()`.\n\nThe cause is in the tests. While the clock scenario drops the lease and takes it again, s0 runs on another thread of the same test process and starts a `git` process (`git -C / rev-parse --show-toplevel`). The new process briefly holds a copy of the lock file, so taking the lease back fails. A small Rust program with no app code shows the same thing. Out of 20,000 lock retakes it failed 0 times on its own, and 57 and then 122 times with a thread starting `git`.\n\nThe real app takes the lease once and never takes it again, so users are not affected. CI runs every library test in one process, and many of them start `git`, so CI may fail more often than this Mac does. That is an inference; I did not measure it on CI. The fix is test-only: retry `start` in `World::restart` for a bounded time.\n\n**The other three minors:**\n- **Real-DB copies.** The clock scenario fails on a real database copy once this PR''s app has ru"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-5c377142-4571-49c9-bac9-e705578a567d', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792453333663, "durationMs": 2449685}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Round 2 of #142 is clean at `9a1cee9d5678f843a3bbaad5049b4bebec80cfa3`, with no blocker, major or minor. M1, m1, m2 and m3 are fixed, and I dropped m4. `review/agent` is now success on that SHA. The review is at `change-r/reviews/r3-round2.md` and is also posted as https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/142#issuecomment-6280333051. No model money was spent. The head did not move during the review.\n\nFor whoever runs R3 next, the harness''s waits now end on the condition they watch, and a wait that never succeeds fails with a clear message. It also cannot write outside `/tmp`. For the next person who owns `active_clock.rs`, a clock gives one `Reading` from one `now()` call, so there is no call-order rule left to break.\n\n**M1.** I tested `wait_until`, `holds` and `q` straight from the pinned `app.sh` under the Mac''s bash 3.2. A condition that is never true failed with `timed out after 4 s` and exit 2 after 4 s. One that became true after 2.5 s returned after 3 s. I built the head with the fake clock under `heavy-lock.sh` and ran S0 through the real app. It passed all ten checks in 25 s, including 60000 ms active after +60 s, the same value after `kill -9`, two `app_down` rows, and one prompt per puppet rule.\n\n**m1.** I gave it a HOME or an OUT folder outside `/tmp` with `s0`, `s0-run` and `build`. All six runs exited 2 before any write, and no folder was created.\n\n**m2.** I made a copy of the puppet with every comment line deleted and the `fx acp` text taken out of "}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-6050579f-6bc4-42d4-9901-768d02acaa3a', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792458578462, "durationMs": 3757216}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I reviewed #150 at `464c34c2` and the verdict is **changes needed**: 0 blockers, 0 majors, 4 minors and 3 nits. I ran none of the PR''s tests, scenarios, real-DB copies or mutations myself. Another session''s ADE-53 stack run held the heavy lock for the whole review. My locked run waited 49 minutes without starting, so I stopped it. Every finding rests on quoted code, the PR''s own tests, or the builder''s own mutation table. Each claim that needs a run is marked `[unproven]`.\n\nThe review is at `change-r/reviews/r5a-round1.md`. It is also posted as a summary comment (https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/150#issuecomment-6281714296) with 6 line comments on the pinned SHA. `review/agent` failure is posted through `scripts/gate/review-status.sh`.\n\n**What holds up.**\n- **Size.** Part 1 is 380 non-test lines against `f808e4af`, and the ask/ack split is the right line. The only failing check is `gate/integrity`, on the 712-line count from #139, which I confirmed in the CI job log.\n- **Table.** `mono_asks` matches plan section 2 and only adds. Neither real DB has a `mono_ask*` table yet, checked read-only.\n- **Rules.** `is_direct_child` and `decide_ask` are pure. Levels come only from saved rows, and a Mono asking a lead started by another Mono is refused.\n- **Deadline.** The due time reads `mono_clock`, not wall time, and a Giga''s ask gets NULL.\n- **Send path.** `submit_in` is the old `submit` body moved, not copied, so every existing caller behaves the same. The ask id"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792480711476, "durationMs": 6054}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Nothing to do here. The #150 round 1 review stands as delivered, with verdict changes needed (0 blockers, 0 majors, 4 minors) on `464c34c2`."}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-6597e0ac-fdb9-4f3b-94db-0f423b5a5332', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792444316210, "durationMs": 1572457}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #141 needs changes: 0 blockers, 1 major, 1 minor. I posted the verdict for `b368e39fc78f9f0a8935b0fce35ac1c788841679` as `review/agent` failure. The head did not move during the review.\n\nThe fix works for the case it targets. When a Mono starts a session and then steers it, the Mono now gets one report, and that report carries the turn''s final answer. But it also loses a report that tyler/main delivers, so it can''t merge as written.\n\n**Major: the duplicate check can lose an accepted report.** The first request to see a session end claims it for its Mono. That claim happens before anyone knows whether the request was accepted. The second request is then removed from its group (`monoSessionCompletion.ts:213`). If the first request is rejected afterwards, nothing hands the end back, so nobody reports it. I proved this with a throwaway test using the real `createTurnWatchers` and `deliverAppSteer`. One Mono steers a session twice. The turn ends while both steers are still in flight. The first steer then fails and the second succeeds. This PR sends no report (`expected [] to have a length of 1 but got +0`); the base sends one. The race is narrow, and I have not seen it in the real app.\n- On your \"right home\" question: the `WeakMap` belongs in the batches class. It is not React state, it copies nothing from the store, it can''t leak, and two Monos or a later end of the same session each still get their report.\n- But there are now two decisions, not one. The pure `releasableResult"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-69982a01-2c44-4865-a2ee-c432b78b33d7', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792453333532, "durationMs": 2410141}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I reviewed round 3 of #139 at head `f808e4afc5bd8ec250c75fcaca236704ac3f0e95`, and it is clean. There are no blockers, majors or minors. The head did not move during the review, and model spend was zero. Tyler gets the defect from round 2 covered by a test. If a worker that a lead starts cannot be saved, the lead hears that the launch failed and the worker is never sent its prompt. Whoever owns this code next gets one more e7 case written like its neighbours, with no new helper or fixture.\n\nThe fix diff `923eb596..f808e4af` changes only `tests/browser/e7-session-callers.spec.ts`, adding 21 lines and removing none. `integrity.sh` still reports 334 non-test lines, the same as round 2. The new case at `:623` makes `session_upsert` throw for `app-lead-r1` only. It then checks that the lead''s reply is `ok: false` and that `prompts(page, \"app-lead-r1\")` is `[]`. The clean spec passed 10 of 10 (measured). Under my round-2 mutant (`access !== null &&` changed to `access === \"operator\" &&`), the whole spec fails on exactly this case. Repeated 5 times, the mutant failed all 5 runs at line 638 with `- Array []` against `+ Array [ \"Write the tests\" ]` (measured). That is the expected mismatch, not a crash. I restored the worktree after each mutant.\n\nAll 14 CI checks pass on this head. `tyler/main` is still `d29fea87`, which is an ancestor of the head, and `git merge-tree` merges it with no conflicts (tree `c5a81e36`). I did not grade the two new user-visible strings, as the brief says. T"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-7014834f-8426-4e5b-ba55-813833c0b1ac', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792452393545, "durationMs": 1136564}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #141 at `f60c19e6` still needs changes. I found 0 blockers, 0 majors, 1 minor and 2 nits. Both round 1 findings are fixed, but a new test gap stops a clean verdict. The verdict is posted as `review/agent` failure on that exact SHA, alongside one line comment and the summary comment. The review is in `change-linear/reviews/ade47-round2.md`, and the probes and raw output are in `ade47-r2-probes/`. No model spend.\n\n**Round 1 MAJOR (one decision).** Fixed. Settle now only records the result and its end, and the pure `releasableResults` makes the decision. `release` marks an end as given only for members it hands to `onReady`, and those are always accepted. I ran five orderings with the real `createTurnWatchers` and `deliverAppSteer`, and each gave exactly one report per Mono:\n- my round 1 race (the first steer fails after the turn ends, the second lands);\n- the reverse (the accepted steer is decided first, then the other is rejected);\n- three steers in flight;\n- three requests across three groups;\n- two Monos racing.\n\nA later end of the same session still reports. The committed race test fails both ways it should. Removing the dedupe inside one release fails it, and letting a discarded request count its end fails it too, which was round 1''s bug.\n\n**Mutation 4.** It can''t be seen from outside the class. The stuck member counts as ready, so its group never waits on it, and it can never send because it keeps its own end in the \"already reported\" set. The only cost is memory. The "}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-7f31a238-6172-443e-b0b9-cac4fd06c5c0', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792449190521, "durationMs": 1844913}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I reviewed #142 at the pinned head `118c3791bba66d4b01f1960a66426c1ea6086334` and it needs changes: 0 blockers, 1 major and 4 minors. The head did not move during the review. I posted five line comments, the summary comment and `review/agent` = failure for that SHA. The nits are in https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/issues/149, and the review is in `change-r/reviews/r3-round1.md`.\n\nThe next R-series builders depend on this harness to drive the real app with no model spend. For them, the clock half works: in my run the file clock moved the app''s active clock and the value survived a `kill -9`. The weak spot is the harness''s only wait, which never waits.\n\n**Findings.**\n- **M1, major.** `wait_for` (`app.sh:31`) never waits on macOS. Its pattern `^(0|)$` does not compile, so the test exits with code 2 for every input, and `|| return 0` reads that as \"done\" on the first try. The timeout path can never run. S0 only passes because a fixed `sleep 5` happens to be long enough on this Mac today. I confirmed it with `/bin/bash -c ''[[ 0 =~ ^(0|)$ ]]; echo $?''`, which prints `2`, and with a `bash -x` trace.\n- **m1.** `run_s0` is called inside `|| …`, which turns off stop-on-error for the whole function. I ran it with `GIGA_EVAL_HOME` set to a scratch folder outside `/tmp`, then deleted the folder. The `/tmp` guard refused it three times, but the run still wrote `.local/bin/fx`, `.puppet/` and a DB under that folder and carried on. With default settings nothing goes outside `/"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-9a5779e8-6424-40f8-9ff2-ca1e415d3324', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792470377123, "durationMs": 916645}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Verdict on #153 at `2250e869f9225cd0910026ef154e5de535118dc9`: changes needed, with 0 blockers, 0 majors and 1 minor. `review/agent` now shows failure on that sha. The head never moved during the review.\n\nWhoever owns the GigaEval harness gets one fix to make before this merges. n7 is done. `advance` has no fixed sleep left. After each clock write it waits up to 30 s for the stored `active_ms` to reach the step''s value. I ran a never-true advance, and it stopped with `timed out after 30 s: the app stores active_ms 20000` and exit 2 after 31 s. When the value did arrive after 4 s, `advance` returned at 4 s.\n\nn8 is almost done. There is now one guard in `lib.sh`, placed before its `mkdir`, and both old copies are deleted. Every unsafe path I tried was refused and nothing got created. That covered the review''s `/tmp/../../Users/...` case, `..` in the part not yet created, a `/tmp` symlink to `$HOME` (as OUT and as HOME), plain home paths with and without spaces, bare `/tmp/`, and a relative path run from `/Users/Shared`. `/private/tmp`, `/tmp` and space paths were allowed, as they should be. An empty value falls back to the `/tmp` default.\n\nThe minor is that the guard accepts a relative path when you run from a folder under `/tmp`. Both old guards refused that. The guard also throws away the resolved path it computes, so the scripts keep the raw relative value. `instance.sh start` then reads that value after it changes folder, so `rel-out/instance.log` cannot be found and the ap"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-a96524bb-b136-400a-ab82-06455ac93608', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792432132817, "durationMs": 925451}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #134 is clean at the pinned head `58bddd6c`. I found no blockers, majors or minors, and two nits.\n\n**Real data.** I took a fresh read-only copy of the real DB and replayed it through the real Rust loaders and the app''s `getSession`. That gave the builder''s before/after numbers to the millisecond. It also turned up a third inverted turn the recheck didn''t have: `m_nrw3tsvkuxzg`, sent at 21:33 last night. In the live DB it has no duration, so the bug hit the shipped app again after the recheck. In replay this PR fixes it.\n\nAcross every turn start in both DBs, the new rule picks a different message than the old one only for these three turns. Everywhere else it picks the same one. The real data has no message without a start time out of 1,599, so the \"no start time still counts at its place\" rule never fires in practice. The PR needs it only to keep an existing test passing.\n\n**What I checked against the brief:**\n- **Callers.** There is no language server for this repo, so I found the callers by text search. There are four direct ones, and the indirect paths include every place that ends a turn. The composer''s own copy of the rule is gone, so one function now decides the opener everywhere.\n- **Tests.** The three new tests fail on `tyler/main` with the expected wrong values (for example `expected 4000 to be 5000`). The builder''s three test-breaking checks give the same results when I re-run them. I added seven scenarios of my own:\n  - a steer sent last\n  - a waiting queued mes"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-a9dfb5b4-1db8-4f11-bcee-29deafcfca5f', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792466781791, "durationMs": 746824}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Round 2 on #150 (head `977da383`) is **changes needed: 0 blockers, 0 majors, 2 minors, 3 nits**. All four round-1 minors are fixed in the code. One half of the m2 fix has no test, and the PR body contradicts itself.\n\n**What''s fixed.** Each item below was checked by my own runs, one lock per command.\n- **m1.** The new fault test catches the missing transaction. With the transaction removed it fails on the row count (`left: 1, right: 0`), not on a compile error.\n- **m2.** An ask that reuses a plain send''s request id now gets `request_id_reused` and writes no ask row. A repeat of a real ask still comes back as a duplicate.\n- **m3.** A caller or target with no saved row now gets the send gate''s own answer (`unknown_caller` or `unknown_target`).\n- **m4.** 9 and 481 are refused, and 10 and 480 are accepted.\n\n**Brief question 4 (the listed but unsaved session).** `not_direct_child` is the right answer, and `unknown_target` would be wrong. The send gate does find that session, and a plain send to it succeeds. So \"No Mono or project session with that id\" would contradict a send that works one call later. The app writes a Mono''s `start_session` row only after the started session is saved. So in the normal flow, a listed session with no saved row was not started by that Mono, and \"not your direct child\" is true.\n\nThere is one edge case, which I filed as nit n2 because I could not prove the app reaches it. A lead that the Mono started in a folder the app never saves (`~`, a remote projec"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-b4677bea-0d5c-4efe-b570-6a3d7a1eab69', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792427420784, "durationMs": 4256840}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "R2 is open as https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/132 at head `aac6d80e`, with the Linear link to ADE-20. All 14 CI checks pass, including `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity`. It is not merged. One clock-scenario failure is still unexplained (see the end).\n\n**What it does**\n- **`active_clock.rs` (new):** one stored `mono_clock` row counts awake, running time, and a new `mono_downtime` table records why time was not counted.\n  - **On launch,** the clock goes on from the stored value. The gap since the last write adds nothing and is logged as `reboot` or `app_down`.\n  - **Every 1 s tick** adds at most 30 s of uptime. A gap of 2 s or more between the monotonic clock and uptime is logged as `sleep`. The row is written every 5 s of active time and after a sleep.\n  - **Lease:** only the process holding the lock on `scheduler.lock` runs the clock. A second process on the same data folder writes nothing.\n  - The 1 s thread starts beside `reminders::init`. No `Instant` or Tokio timer is used.\n- **World:** a `FakeClock` replaces R1''s plain `now`. It offers `advance`, `stall`, `sleep`, `jump_wall`, `down` and `reboot`, and the World relaunches the clock through the same `start` function the app calls.\n\n**Linux and Windows clocks:** confirmed from the documentation, and the code compiles for both. I could not run a real sleep on either.\n\n| OS | Clock that stops during sleep | Clock that counts sleep | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Linux | `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` | `CLOCK_BOOTTIME`"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792432970665, "durationMs": 1726051}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "All four round 1 minors on #132 are fixed in one push. The new head is `d6596fc1`. All 14 CI checks pass on the first run, `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity` included. The PR is not merged.\n\n**Fixes**\n- **m1 (flake):** `World::restart` now retries taking the lease every 1 ms for up to 2 s, then fails with the lease error. `second_launch` still tries once. The app is unaffected, because it takes the lease once and never retakes it.\n  - Close-on-exec check: the lock file already has `FD_CLOEXEC`. A throwaway `fcntl(F_GETFD)` probe printed `FD_CLOEXEC set: true`. So close-on-exec is not the gap; the race sits between a child''s fork and its exec, which close-on-exec cannot cover.\n- **m2 (real-DB copies with a clock):** the scenario now notes the copy''s last downtime row id after its own launch, and checks only rows after it.\n- **m3 (stall logged as sleep):** after the 90 s stall, the scenario asserts there is no new downtime row (\"a stall is no sleep\").\n- **m4 (lease could be dropped):** `ActiveClock` now owns its `Lease`, and `start` returns `Option<ActiveClock>`. `init` holds no separate lease, so the reviewer''s `let _ = lease;` line no longer exists to mutate. The closest equivalent, starting the clock after dropping the lock, is caught.\n\n**Parallel runs** (the reviewer''s script: 8 test processes at once, prebuilt binary):\n\n| Binary | Both scenarios | Clock alone |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before (`aac6d80e`), 8 × 60 | 475/480, all failures \"another process holds the lease\" | not run |\n|"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792434743093, "durationMs": 5379152}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "The store has no `lead` row, so the call now gets `unknown_caller`. I''ll seed the fixture''s saved sessions the same way the replay test does:"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792440207390, "durationMs": 8913426}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #142 is open. While CI runs, I''ll record the decisions and clean up temp files:"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792451053931, "durationMs": 2248535}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Both fix rounds are pushed and all 14 CI checks pass on each PR. Neither is merged. One thing differs from what you asked: I did not add a CI job for R3 m4, because any workflow edit fails `gate/integrity` without Tyler''s override (details under R3).\n\n**R4, review round 2.** #139 (https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/139) is now at `f808e4af`, with \"Review rounds: 2\".\n- I added one Playwright case to the e7 spec. A lead starts a worker, saving the worker fails, the lead gets `ok: false`, and the worker gets no prompt.\n- Proof, from `r4-r2-mutation.sh` run under the heavy lock: the spec passes 10 of 10 clean. With the wait limited to `/operator` turns (`access === \"operator\"`), the new case fails and the other 9 pass.\n- This round adds a test only, so non-test size is still 334 lines.\n- I replied on the `App.tsx:8232` comment and resolved the thread.\n- The merge still waits on Tyler waiving the 2 new strings.\n\n**R3, review round 1.** #142 (https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/142) is now at `9a1cee9d`, with \"Review rounds: 1\".\n- **M1, the wait.** `wait_for` is replaced by `wait_until`, which retries an exact condition and stops the run with \"timed out after N s\" when time runs out. The fixed `sleep 5`, and the `sleep 3` after the restart, are replaced by waits for real conditions (both messages delivered, the second app-down row). In a probe, a condition that never comes true failed after 3 s with exit 2. Once it was true, the same call returned"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792455862967, "durationMs": 2679452}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I split R5 into two parts because as one PR it was 576 non-test lines, over the 500 cap. Part 1 is open as https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/150 (head `464c34c2`, not merged). Part 2 is pushed as branch `feat/ade-23-ack` (`26475433`) with no PR yet; it opens after #150 merges, under the rule that a stack''s next PR waits for the previous merge.\n\n**CI on #150: 13 of 14 pass; `gate/integrity` fails, as expected.** It reports 712 non-test lines because the diff against `tyler/main` still includes #139, which #150 is built on. Measured against #139''s head, part 1 is 380 lines and passes. Once #139 merges I''ll rebase and the gate should pass. The PR body says \"Depends on #139; merges after it\".\n\n**Part 1 (#150, the ask side)**\n- **Table and gate.** The new `mono_asks` table goes in through the existing migration. Two pure functions, `is_direct_child` and `decide_ask`, decide who may ask whom: Giga to a Mono, Mono to a lead it started, lead to its own worker. Everything else is refused with `not_direct_child`.\n- **Deadlines.** A Giga''s ask gets no deadline. Any other ask is due at the stored active-clock time plus the budget (60 minutes by default, or 10 to 480 if given).\n- **Delivery.** `asks::ask` runs in one transaction. The ask travels as an ordinary `send_message` row to the child, written through the existing send path (split out as `submit_in`). So asks get the same send rules, delivery and request-id dedupe as messages, and the large `mono_messages` table "}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792462372065, "durationMs": 4371744}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Now the gate rewrite (m3 + n3):"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792467543350, "durationMs": 1374373}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Both round-2 minors on #150 are fixed. The PR is at `9fe5dbfd`, marked \"Review rounds: 2\", and not merged: https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/150\n\n**CI:** 13 of 14 checks pass. `gate/integrity` fails because it still counts #139''s lines (733). #139 is still open. Measured against #139''s head, `gate/integrity` passes at 401 non-test lines.\n\n**m1.** I added the test `a_retried_rejected_ask_gets_its_first_refusal`.\n- An ask to the unknown target `r5-nobody` gets `unknown_target`.\n- The same call again, with the same request id, gets `unknown_target` with `duplicate: true`.\n- No `mono_asks` row is written.\n- **Mutant check:** with the rejected-repeat guard deleted, the new test fails: `left: ((\"unknown_target\", false), (\"request_id_reused\", false))`. Without the mutant, all 7 `ask_gate` and `scenario::asks` tests pass. Each run held the lock for that one command.\n\n**m2.** This was a PR-body edit only; the code head did not move for it. These parts now match `9fe5dbfd`:\n- The Proof mutation table says the transaction mutant is caught, and the race explanation is gone.\n- The data-flow block shows the three repeat cases, the parties-with-no-row path and the rollback.\n- The `decide_ask` line is updated.\n- The budget cases are 9, 10, 480 and 481, with 5 asks still open after the kill.\n- Both size mentions now read 401.\n\n**Nits:** n1, n2 and n3 are added to #151 as a comment.\n\n**PR comments:** all 4 round-2 line comments have replies and are resolved.\n\n**Part 2:** `fe"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792468979203, "durationMs": 1377287}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "The PR for nits n7 and n8 is open as https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/153, on `fix/ade-21-harness-nits` from `tyler/main`. The head is `2250e869`, all 14 CI checks pass, and it is not merged. The diff is 49 changed lines, all shell (`gate/integrity` ok), and the body says \"Review rounds: 0\".\n\nOne difference from your wording: the shared guard file is `scripts/giga-eval/lib.sh`, not `scripts/scenarios/lib.sh`. The scenarios folder has no `lib.sh`, and `app.sh` sources the giga-eval one.\n\n**n7: the advance wait.** `advance` no longer sleeps a fixed 1.5 s after each clock write. It now waits up to 30 s until the stored active clock reaches the value that step should give, and stops the run with \"timed out\" if it doesn''t. If two writes ever land in one tick, the app caps the jump at 30 s, so the run now times out right there instead of failing a later check by 10 s.\n\n**n8: the `/tmp` guard.** There is now one guard in `lib.sh`, before its `mkdir`. It checks both `GIGA_EVAL_OUT` and `GIGA_EVAL_HOME`, so every GigaEval script is covered.\n- It resolves the deepest folder that already exists with `cd -P`, which follows symlinks and `..`.\n- It refuses any `..` in the part not yet created, since `cd -P` can''t see that part.\n- The result must be under `/tmp`.\n- I deleted the old copies in `app.sh` and `instance.sh` (`guard_home`).\n- It only checks; scripts keep the paths they were given.\n\n**Proof:**\n- **`/tmp/../..` paths:** `GIGA_EVAL_OUT=/tmp/../../Users/tylerstewa"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792471308104, "durationMs": 1174416}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I fixed m1 on #153 (https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/153). The new head is `d13e4825`, all 14 CI checks pass, the body says \"Review rounds: 1\", and it is not merged.\n\n**The fix.** I chose to refuse rather than resolve. The guard in `lib.sh` now rejects any `GIGA_EVAL_OUT` or `GIGA_EVAL_HOME` that is not an absolute path. The message reads \"must be absolute paths that resolve under /tmp\". This way the guard still only checks, and no script''s path is changed behind its back. It is 2 lines more, and `gate/integrity` now reports 51 non-test lines.\n\n**Proof.** I ran the reviewer''s `n7n8-round1-probes.sh` on the old head `2250e869` and on the fix, then diffed the two outputs.\n- \"Relative path, run from a /tmp folder\" used to exit 0 and create the folder. It now exits 2 and creates nothing.\n- `under_tmp \"\"` and `under_tmp rel`, run from a /tmp folder, used to return 0. They now return 1.\n- Apart from the new message text, nothing else changed:\n  - Every case that was refused before (`/tmp/..` traversal, symlinks to the home folder, plain home paths, bare `/tmp/`, relative paths from `/Users/Shared`) still exits 2 with nothing created.\n  - The absolute `/tmp`, `/private/tmp`, path-with-spaces and empty-default cases still exit 0.\n  - The dangling-symlink cases (n1) behave as before.\n- Both outputs are saved in `r3-files/n7n8-r1-probes-old.out` and `n7n8-r1-probes-new.out`.\n\nI did not rerun S0 through the real app this round. The change only adds a refusal, and S0 "}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792473481689, "durationMs": 3693609}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792477176061, "durationMs": 2295718}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "ADE-58 did not reproduce in the real Linux app: 0 hangs in 60 two-player runs and 0 in 20 one-video control runs. My verdict is **no on this stack, unproven elsewhere**: no hang on Ubuntu 24.04 with WebKitGTK 2.52.6 and GStreamer 1.24.2, while another distro or WebKit version could still behave like the Playwright WPE build did.\n\nThe `scratch/ade-58-repro` branch is deleted locally and on GitHub, along with its worktree. No PR was opened and no product code changed.\n\n**How it ran.** The real app ran in CI, so no fallback to a plain WebKitGTK page was needed.\n- A temporary workflow built the release Linux binary and ran it under Xvfb, driven through tauri-driver (WebKitWebDriver).\n- Each run used a fresh HOME and a project folder holding `clip.mp4` and `other.mp4`, copied from the test fixture (a 3 s H.264 clip).\n- My first attempts showed no video elements, because the app saved its own workspace over the one I set from the page. So each run writes the workspace into the instance database between two launches.\n- After the app restored the video tabs, the driver pinged the web process every 2 s for 60 s. A ping with no answer in 10 s would have counted as a hang.\n\n**Results** (run 39380281417, 20 runs per shape):\n\n| Shape | Runs | Hangs | Dropped connections | Players loaded (readyState 3) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Two video tabs in one pane | 20 | **0** | 2 | both, in all 20 |\n| One video in each split pane | 20 | **0** | 2 | both, in all 20 |\n| One visible workspace tab, one"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792480746148, "durationMs": 57218}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "ADE-58 already finished before the relaunch, so there was nothing left to resume. My last `finished.log` line (17:57) records it as done, and I checked that `scratch/ade-58-repro` no longer exists on GitHub. The saved evidence is still in place.\n\nThe output above counts one local `scratch/*` branch, so the local delete is not confirmed. I didn''t check whether that branch is the ADE-58 one; it could also be another session''s scratch branch. `git branch -D scratch/ade-58-repro` removes it if it is.\n\n**Verdict: no on this stack, unproven elsewhere.** The hang did not reproduce in the real Linux app on Ubuntu 24.04 with WebKitGTK 2.52.6 and GStreamer 1.24.2. A different distro or WebKit version could still hang the way Playwright''s WPE build did in ADE-49''s tests.\n\n**How it was tested.** No fallback was needed: CI drove the real Linux app.\n- A temporary workflow built the release Linux app and ran it under Xvfb, driven through tauri-driver (WebKitWebDriver).\n- Each run used a fresh data folder and a project with `clip.mp4` and `other.mp4`.\n- After the video tabs were restored, the web process was pinged every 2 s for 60 s. A ping with no answer in 10 s would have counted as a hang.\n\n**Counts** (20 runs per shape):\n\n| Shape | Hangs | Dropped connections | Both players loaded (readyState 3) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Two video tabs in one pane | 0 / 20 | 2 | 20 / 20 |\n| One video in each split pane | 0 / 20 | 2 | 20 / 20 |\n| One visible workspace tab, one hidden | 0 / 20 | 1 | 20 / 20 |"}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792480939478, "durationMs": 972811}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "R5 part 2 is open as #155, \"ADE-23 R5 part 2: ack ledger\": https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/155. The head is `0b3d523a` on `tyler/main` `ed381066`. All 14 CI checks pass, including `gate/lint` and `gate/integrity`. It is not merged.\n\n- **Branch head:** you named `a747d808`, but my local `feat/ade-23-ack` was already at `53513cda`. That is the same ack commit, rebased onto #150''s last head before it merged. I rebased that onto `ed381066`; it applied cleanly with no code change.\n- **Integrity:** `scripts/gate/integrity.sh ed381066 HEAD` gives `ok, 212 non-test lines changed`, and CI''s `gate/integrity` reports the same. The diff is +591/-10 in 5 files. The non-test lines are `asks.rs` +147/-7, `ask_gate.rs` +55/-2 and `mono_messages.rs` +1.\n- **Tests:** each run held its own lock. `cargo test --lib -- ask_gate scenario::` passed all 17. `repeat.sh asks::s2_two_acks` passed 20/20.\n- **PR body:** it comes from `r5-part2-pr-body.md`, with \"Review rounds: 0\", the ADE-23 link, and the new base, size and CI result. I also fixed one stale line that still called the ask-transaction mutant a survivor; it has been caught since #150''s round 1.\n- **Mutations:** the ack mutation results in the body come from a run before part 1''s review rounds. The body says so, and the ack code has not changed since.\n\nThe decision is appended to `decisions.md`. The finish ping failed with `turn_inactive`, and I logged it in `finished.log`."}, {"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792482324959, "durationMs": 2236387}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Meanwhile, recording the decisions:"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-d003b5df-9eb5-402a-bc6c-99bf29bfae5f', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792472499673, "durationMs": 189431}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "PR #153 at `d13e4825` is clean: no blockers, majors or minors. `review/agent` on that SHA is now success.\n\nhttps://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/153\n\n**Brief items:**\n\n1. **Fix diff.** The diff `2250e869..d13e4825` is 3 lines added and 1 removed, all in `scripts/giga-eval/lib.sh`. Line 20 now refuses any path that does not start with `/`, before anything resolves it. `GIGA_EVAL_OUT` and `GIGA_EVAL_HOME` both pass through that one guard, so both are covered. Both old guards refused relative paths too, so this is the round-1 minor fixed the way round 1 suggested.\n2. **Round-1 probes.** I re-ran `n7n8-round1-probes.sh` unchanged on the new head. The relative-path case from a `/tmp` folder now exits 2 with nothing created. The only other changes are `under_tmp \"\"` and `under_tmp \"rel\"` from that folder, which now fail (rc 1). The other 22 results match round 1; only the error text differs, and that change is intended. Extra probes in `n7n8-round2-extra.sh` also exit 2 with nothing created: a relative HOME, `./dot-out`, and relative OUT and HOME through `app.sh s0 1`.\n3. **Empty value.** `lib.sh:5-6` swaps an empty value for the default before the guard runs, so an empty value is never refused. `instance.sh status` exits 0 with both values empty, with only HOME empty, and with both unset. Sourcing `lib.sh` with both empty gives `EVAL_OUT=/tmp/giga-eval EVAL_HOME=/tmp/giga-eval-home`.\n4. **CI and merge.** All 14 checks pass on `d13e4825`. `tyler/main` is still `f9250"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-d4d0ba0f-98b7-4294-887a-44d05c2bdca3', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792468947071, "durationMs": 620131}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "Round 3 of #150 comes back **changes needed** for `9fe5dbfd312d95a5c1d7040845d6a7fbf901eb31`. The test fix is done, but one stale line is still in the PR body. That gives 0 blockers, 0 majors and 1 minor. I wrote the review to `change-r/reviews/r5a-round3.md`, posted it as [a comment on the PR](https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/150#issuecomment-6283196848), and posted the `review/agent` failure status on that SHA with `review-status.sh`.\n\n- **m1 is fixed.** The fix diff from `977da383` is one new test, `a_retried_rejected_ask_gets_its_first_refusal`, and no product code. It checks that the retry gets the same `unknown_target` code with `duplicate: true` and that no `mono_asks` row is written. I deleted the rejected-repeat guard and ran the tests again. Only this test failed, on its own assertion: `left: ((\"unknown_target\", false), (\"request_id_reused\", false))`. I then restored `asks.rs` and confirmed it matched the original exactly.\n- **m2 is fixed except for one line.** These parts of the body now match the head:\n  - the Proof table (the transaction mutant is now caught and the race text is gone);\n  - the data-flow block, which I checked against `asks.rs` lines 104 to 180;\n  - the `decide_ask` line;\n  - the 9/10/480/481 budget cases and the 5 open asks;\n  - both mentions of 401 lines. The per-file breakdown agrees with `git diff --numstat` and adds up to 401.\n\n  One line is still stale. The \"File caps\" bullet says `asks.rs` 220 lines and `ask_gate.rs` 170"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-e948d922-0ea2-41b1-8935-6758ba62fbf9', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792449170407, "durationMs": 1670612}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "The verdict for `923eb596` is changes needed, with 0 blockers, 0 majors and 1 minor. The full review is in `change-r/reviews/r4-round2.md` and is also posted as a comment on https://github.com/tylers2222/Monocode-Unlocked/pull/139.\n\nAll five round-1 findings are fixed.\n\n- **`/operator` in `~`.** A typed `/operator` in a `~` or remote session is now refused before any turn starts. The decision is in the pure `submitAccess` function and nowhere else. I re-ran my round-1 probes on this head. Rust still refuses a session with no saved row (`unknown_caller`, new hint, no row written). On the web side, `/operator` in `~` gives `\"unsaved\"`, and an older `~` thread that already has an `/operator` block gives `null`, so it gets no app access.\n- **Waiting for the save.** A turn with app access now waits for its session to be saved, and a failed save stops the turn. This uses the same kind of `await` as the steer path. It adds no new effect, ref or timer. `sessions.start` now answers the lead only after the worker''s row is saved.\n- **Other ways in.** Mono chats are not affected. Habit runs keep their access: both real databases have 48 habit-run rows and none of them is in `~`. Orchestration runs, remote sessions and restored tabs do not give app access to a session with no row. The one loose end is that compacting a session''s context does not apply the new rule (nit n6). It is harmless today because compaction runs no tools [inference].\n- **Approvals.** The rebase did not change what a"}]');
INSERT INTO sessions VALUES ('app-86984142-5f1a-4018-af62-ddb105cb6cb4-ee8af85e-fdb5-47b3-bf9e-f2cc334dffd8', '[{"role": "user", "startedAt": 1792480727852, "durationMs": 826512}, {"role": "assistant", "text": "I wrote the Ship 5 check to `change-linear/reviews/ship5-check.md`. Each ticket has a verdict, the query I ran and the row values behind it. Everything came from read-only copies of both databases under `/tmp` plus the omp and claude transcripts, with no clicks and no model spend. The last copy was taken at 18:30:17. The probe scripts are in `reviews/ship5-check-probes/`, and `all.sh` takes fresh copies and reruns all of them.\n\n**Cutoff.** I used 17:19:33, not 17:19:47. `ps` shows the new apps started at 17:19:33 and 17:19:34, and four sessions were auto-resumed on the new build at 17:19:36. With 17:19:47 those turns would have been left out.\n\n**Verdicts**\n- **ADE-15: PASS.** In the Fork Orchestrator chat, every session it started or sent to that has finished got exactly one notice. That covers 4 sessions: 872c4aa7 and 7a1eab69 in one combined notice, e98daa03 and 3ef47519 alone.\n  - The 60 s hold worked. Its turn ended at 18:19:15.456 and the combined notice opened at 18:20:15.478.\n  - The two single notices waited until the Orchestrator was idle (14 ms and 9 ms after its turn ended) instead of interrupting it.\n  - The \"noop\" session 02acaa3a got no notice. That is correct: the Orchestrator stopped it, and stopping a session drops its notice (`App.tsx:11616-11640`).\n  - Notices for this check session and two other sessions still running are pending.\n- **ADE-47: PASS.** No session end was reported twice to the same Mono. The exact case it fixes, a message steered into a turn "}]');
